Investigate the fullscreen image quality issue from source to display. Do not assume the camera is the problem.

I compared the same kind of photo outside BlueMind and inside BlueMind. The original photo is sharp, but BlueMind fullscreen preview looks visibly degraded/blurry.

The original test image is high enough resolution to look sharp on an iPhone. Therefore, investigate the complete image pipeline and find exactly where quality is lost.

Trace:

camera/photo source
→ original asset/file
→ attachment object
→ thumbnail generation
→ any resize/compression
→ any base64 conversion
→ any UIImage/data conversion
→ preview source
→ fullscreen renderer

At every stage, log/report:

* pixel width × height;
* file size;
* MIME/format;
* whether it is original or derived;
* whether compression/downsampling happened.

Specifically check whether fullscreen preview is accidentally using:

* the composer thumbnail;
* a low-resolution cached image;
* a resized preview image;
* a canvas snapshot;
* a compressed JPEG generated for UI;
* a base64 version with reduced quality;
* UIImage decoding with downsampling;
* a browser/WebView representation instead of the original native file.

Do not fix this by simply adding sharpening, fake 4K upscaling, or CSS filters.

The correct fix is to make fullscreen preview render the original/highest-resolution image source.

Keep a separate lightweight thumbnail only for the composer.

Fullscreen preview must never reuse that thumbnail.

If the current iOS preview architecture cannot display the original image cleanly, rebuild the iOS preview path using native iOS image rendering and the original local asset/file.

Prefer native UIImage / UIImageView / SwiftUI image rendering with proper aspect-fit and zoom behavior.

Do not add a third-party library unless native APIs genuinely cannot solve it.

Also verify interpolation/rendering:

* no blur layer over the image;
* no low-quality scaling;
* no unnecessary rasterization;
* preserve aspect ratio;
* preserve original resolution;
* zoom should reveal real detail from the original source.

Acceptance requirement:

Open the same image in Photos and BlueMind side by side. BlueMind should look essentially as sharp as Photos at normal viewing scale.

Before marking complete, report the exact stage where resolution was lost and the before/after pixel dimensions.
